= Scribunto =

'''Scribunto''' is a MediaWiki extension that embeds a scripting runtime into wiki pages. Although the framework was designed to support scripting languages generally, its production language is [[Lua]].

Scribunto gives MediaWiki a `Module:` namespace for executable code and the `#invoke` parser function for calling exported module functions from wikitext.

== Execution model ==

A module returns a Lua table containing functions or values. Public functions can be called from a wiki page or template through `#invoke`, while modules can compose other modules using Lua `require()`.

The host exposes MediaWiki-aware libraries under `mw`. These provide controlled access to parser frames, titles, text, HTML construction, language functions, URI handling, logging, and extension-specific libraries.

Scribunto executes under resource and security constraints. It is not equivalent to running an unrestricted local Lua interpreter with arbitrary filesystem, process, or network access.

== Why it exists ==

Wikitext templates and ParserFunctions can express conditional and reusable behavior, but large procedural template systems become difficult to maintain and can be expensive to parse. Scribunto moves non-trivial reusable logic into a real programming language while preserving templates as the wikitext-facing interface.

== Scribunto in BITwiki ==

BITwiki treats Scribunto as part of its programmable knowledge substrate. Wikitext remains authoring syntax; Semantic MediaWiki and Cargo provide structured wiki state; Scribunto/Lua performs deterministic normalization, type checking, bounded inference, transformation, and semantic presentation.

This role is specified in [[BITwiki:Lua architecture]] and [[BITwiki:Programmable knowledge substrate]].

BIThub/Discourse may consume views generated from this substrate through integration surfaces, but Scribunto itself is not a cross-service transport or background agent runtime.

